  "content": {
    "id": "p2-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Find the coordinates of the points of intersection of $4y=x^2-4$ and $2y-x=10$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(-4,3)$ and $(6,8)$.",
      "worked": "From $2y-x=10$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2y&=x+10\\\\y&=\\frac{x}{2}+5\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute into $4y=x^2-4$:\n$$\\begin{align*}4\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+5\\right)&=x^2-4\\\\2x+20&=x^2-4\\\\x^2-2x-24&=0\\\\(x-6)(x+4)&=0\\\\x&=6\\text{ or }-4\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x=6$, $y=\\frac{6}{2}+5=8$.\n\nFor $x=-4$, $y=\\frac{-4}{2}+5=3$.\n\nThe intersection points are $(-4,3)$ and $(6,8)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
